#version 450

// Phase 6, step 1 -- a hash RNG in the shader.
//
// The first step of the renderer port, and it deliberately produces no
// geometry. There is still no camera, no ray and no sphere. What changes is
// that the shader can now ask for a random number, which is the one thing a
// path tracer cannot do without and the one thing the CPU's sampling code
// cannot simply be recompiled into.
//
// WHY mt19937 CANNOT COME ALONG. include/raytracer/core/rng.h holds a
// std::mt19937 and a std::uniform_real_distribution per RNG instance. The
// Mersenne Twister's state is 624 32-bit words -- about 2.5 KB. The CPU hands
// out one instance per pixel-sample in deterministic mode, which costs
// nothing when there are eight threads. Here there are 480,000 invocations,
// and 480,000 x 2.5 KB is 1.2 GB of state that would have to be initialised
// before a single sample could be taken. Register and local memory budgets
// are measured in tens of kilobytes per workgroup. It is not slow; it does
// not fit.
//
// So the generator is replaced by a HASH: a function with no state beyond one
// 32-bit word, which is advanced and scrambled on each call. The scheme used
// here is a small linear congruential step followed by a permuting output
// function -- the PCG construction, which is what GPU renderers have settled
// on because the arithmetic is five integer operations and the output passes
// statistical tests that cheaper hashes fail badly.
//
// WHAT CARRIES OVER UNCHANGED is the SEEDING. pixelSampleSeed below is a
// transliteration of pixel_sample_seed in rng.h, operator for operator,
// because that function already derives its randomness purely from the pixel
// coordinates and the sample index -- which is exactly the property a shader
// needs, where there is nowhere to keep a stream between invocations. Only
// the engine changed; what the engine is started from did not.
//
// THE SEEDING SCHEME HAS A KNOWN FLAW AND IT IS PORTED ANYWAY. The three
// inputs are combined with XOR, and XOR of scaled indices is not injective:
// i*1973 ^ j*9277 collides. At 800x600, 26,460 of 480,000 pixels share a seed
// with some other pixel -- 5.5% of them -- so in deterministic mode those
// pairs draw the identical sample sequence. The host checkpoint measures and
// prints that number rather than hiding it. It is ported verbatim because
// this step replaces the ENGINE, not the seeding, and because the scheme is
// shared with the CPU backend: diverging here would quietly break the
// property that both backends walk the same sample sequence, which is what
// the end-of-phase comparison relies on. Fixing it belongs upstream, in one
// change, to both backends at once.

layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8, local_size_z = 1) in;

// Unchanged from Phase 5. Now carries noise rather than a gradient; the
// format qualifier, the writeonly promise and the reasoning behind both are
// the same, and the notes on them are in the Phase 5 history.
layout(binding = 0, rgba8) uniform writeonly image2D outImage;

// A SHADER STORAGE BUFFER, and the first one in this project. The storage
// image above and this are the two ways a compute shader writes bulk data
// out, and they are not interchangeable:
//
//   image2D (binding 0)             buffer (binding 1)
//   texel coordinates               a linear index
//   fixed to a format qualifier     whatever type is declared
//   8 bits per channel here         full 32-bit floats
//   clamps on store (UNORM)         stores the bits given
//   can be sampled by a later pass  cannot be sampled
//
// The last two rows are why this exists. The checkpoint for this step
// compares the shader's floats against the identical arithmetic on the host,
// and a comparison through an 8-bit UNORM image could not do it: the image
// has 256 distinguishable values per channel, it CLAMPS anything outside
// [0,1] instead of reporting it, and its float-to-byte rounding has an
// implementation-chosen tie rule. A buffer of floats has none of those
// properties -- the bits that arrive are the bits the shader computed.
//
// std430, not std140. For a BUFFER block the tighter of the two layouts is
// available, and the difference is real: in std140 an array of float has a
// stride of 16 bytes, in std430 it has a stride of 4. vec4 is laid out
// identically under both, which is the deliberate reason this is a vec4 array
// and not a struct -- there is no layout question to get wrong. Step 2 brings
// back a UNIFORM block, where only std140 is available, and that is where the
// padding rules start to bite.
layout(binding = 1, std430) writeonly buffer RngProbe {
    vec4 draws[];
} probe;

layout(push_constant) uniform PushConstants {
    float time;
} push;

// THE WHOLE GENERATOR STATE: one 32-bit word, against mt19937's 624.
//
// A global in GLSL is per-invocation, not shared -- there is no such thing as
// a variable shared across a dispatch without declaring it so. Every
// invocation has its own copy, seeded from its own coordinates, which is the
// shape the CPU achieves with one RNG object per pixel-sample.
uint rngState;

// ONE STEP OF THE STREAM.
//
// Line 1 is a linear congruential generator: state = state * A + C. On its
// own an LCG is poor -- the low bits have short periods, and consecutive
// outputs fall on a small number of hyperplanes. It is kept because it is one
// multiply and one add and it visits every one of the 2^32 states before
// repeating.
//
// Lines 2 and 3 are the PERMUTING OUTPUT FUNCTION, and they are what make it
// usable. The state is xor-shifted by an amount DERIVED FROM ITS OWN HIGH
// BITS -- a data-dependent rotation, which is the trick the construction is
// named for -- then multiplied by a second odd constant and xor-shifted once
// more. The effect is that the weak low bits of the LCG never reach the
// output, and a one-bit change in the state changes about half the output
// bits.
//
// Unsigned overflow WRAPS and that is relied on, not tolerated: both
// multiplies are modulo 2^32 by design. GLSL defines uint overflow as
// wrapping, as does C++ for unsigned types, so the host reference computes
// the identical value.
//
// What this replaces is worth keeping in view: this function is five integer
// operations and four bytes of state, and it is the entire sampling engine.
uint nextUint() {
    rngState = rngState * 747796405u + 2891336453u;
    uint word = ((rngState >> ((rngState >> 28u) + 4u)) ^ rngState)
                * 277803737u;
    return (word >> 22u) ^ word;
}

// A FLOAT IN [0,1), and the shift is the entire point of the line.
//
// The obvious conversion is float(nextUint()) * (1.0 / 4294967296.0), and it
// is WRONG in a way that no picture could ever show. A float has a 24-bit
// mantissa, so at a magnitude of 2^32 the representable values are 256 apart:
// every uint from 2^32 - 128 upward converts to exactly 2^32, and multiplying
// that by 2^-32 gives exactly 1.0. The function would return 1.0 for about
// one input in 2^25 -- roughly once every 34 million draws, so a render would
// produce it every few seconds and almost no test would ever see it.
//
// That matters because [0,1) is not decoration. A sampler that is handed 1.0
// hits the degenerate end of its mapping: sqrt(1 - r2) in the cosine-weighted
// direction becomes exactly zero, giving a direction in the surface plane, and
// an acos or a 1/(1-r) elsewhere gives infinity or a NaN that propagates
// silently through a pixel's whole path.
//
// Taking the top 24 bits removes the question instead of narrowing it. The
// numerator is below 2^24, so it is EXACTLY representable; the denominator is
// a power of two, so the multiply only adjusts the exponent and rounds
// nothing. The largest result is 16777215/16777216, strictly below 1. There
// is no rounding anywhere in this line, which is also why the host can
// predict every value bit for bit.
//
// std::uniform_real_distribution<double>(0.0, 1.0) on the CPU specifies the
// same half-open interval, so this matches the contract it replaces -- though
// not the values, which no shared contract could give.
float nextFloat() {
    return float(nextUint() >> 8) * (1.0 / 16777216.0);
}

// A TRANSLITERATION of pixel_sample_seed in include/raytracer/core/rng.h.
// Same constants, same operators, same order, same uint32 wraparound. The
// known collision flaw described at the top of the file lives here, and is
// kept deliberately.
// The third parameter is named sampleIndex, not sample. `sample` is a
// RESERVED WORD in GLSL 450 -- it is the interpolation qualifier that forces
// per-sample fragment shading -- so the CPU's parameter name does not survive
// the port. glslc rejects it outright, which is the good case: a reserved
// word that happened to parse would be far worse.
uint pixelSampleSeed(int i, int j, int sampleIndex) {
    uint seed = 0u;
    seed ^= uint(i) * 1973u;
    seed ^= uint(j) * 9277u;
    seed ^= uint(sampleIndex) * 26699u;
    seed += 1u;
    return seed;
}

void main() {
    ivec2 coord = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
    ivec2 size = imageSize(outImage);

    // Still dead at 800x600, still kept. See the Phase 5 note: the resolution
    // is the first thing that will change, and stray invocations writing
    // outside an image are discarded silently rather than diagnosed.
    //
    // It guards the BUFFER as well now, and there the stakes are different. An
    // out-of-range imageStore is defined to be dropped; an out-of-range write
    // through a storage buffer is NOT -- without robustBufferAccess it is
    // undefined behaviour, and with it the write is dropped but the
    // out-of-bounds index is still a bug nothing reports. The one guard
    // covering both is the cheapest version of this that is correct.
    if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y) {
        return;
    }

    // THE SAMPLE INDEX, from the push constant, mirroring Phase 5's shift
    // exactly: both sides truncate the same float product from the same float,
    // so the host reference derives a bit-identical index and the checkpoint
    // holds at an arbitrary animated time rather than only at t = 0.
    //
    // 60 a second, so the noise is replaced about once per displayed frame --
    // fast enough to look like television static rather than a fixed pattern,
    // which is the one thing about this step that is visible.
    //
    // At time == 0 the index is 0, so the startup self-test runs on sample 0
    // with no special case, exactly as the gradient's shift did.
    //
    // This is NOT yet the renderer's sample index. A real sample loop runs
    // many samples per pixel within one frame and averages them, which is
    // Phase 7's accumulation. Here one sample is drawn and shown raw.
    int sampleIndex = int(push.time * 60.0);

    rngState = pixelSampleSeed(coord.x, coord.y, sampleIndex);

    // FOUR DRAWS, in a fixed order, because the order is part of what is
    // being verified. The host reference advances the identical state machine
    // the identical number of times; a shader that drew three or five, or
    // drew them in a different order, would disagree on every value after the
    // first.
    //
    // Four because that is what the sampling code about to be ported actually
    // consumes per bounce: two for the pixel jitter in get_ray, two more for
    // random_cosine_direction. Verifying a stream four deep is verifying the
    // depth the next steps use.
    vec4 v = vec4(nextFloat(), nextFloat(), nextFloat(), nextFloat());

    // THE PROOF. A linear index into the buffer, row-major, matching the
    // host's. Written every frame, which costs 7.68 MB of writes per frame
    // and is accepted deliberately: this buffer exists to be checked, and
    // making it conditional would mean a second code path that the checkpoint
    // does not exercise.
    probe.draws[uint(coord.y) * uint(size.x) + uint(coord.x)] = v;

    // THE PICTURE, from the same four numbers rather than from a second draw,
    // so what is on screen and what is checked cannot disagree. Three
    // channels of independent noise read as colour static; a generator with
    // correlated outputs would show as a grey or tinted cast instead, which
    // is a hint rather than evidence -- the buffer is the evidence.
    //
    // The alpha is 1.0 and not v.w: an image with noisy alpha composites
    // unpredictably, and the fourth draw is in the buffer where it is checked
    // properly.
    imageStore(outImage, coord, vec4(v.x, v.y, v.z, 1.0));
}
